  "slug": "clustering-dark-energy-064b",
  "summary": "Two preprints use nonlinear spherical collapse to show background and linear dark energy parametrizations leave clustering underdetermined.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is nonlinear clustering as a discriminator of dark energy models that share the same background and linear description. One preprint argues that w(a) and a linear sound speed do not fully specify dark energy, and spherical-collapse simulations find central dark-energy behavior that differs between models. A companion preprint introduces a fully nonlinear fluid method that splits the dark energy action into kinetic and potential parts and tests it with spherical-collapse simulations. The new element is this action-level nonlinear treatment as a route to breaking degeneracies. The other two preprints address early-Universe physics: vector perturbations sourced by shear viscosity in warm inflation, and gravitational wave spectra from slowly evolving sources. They are only loosely connected to this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
